__registerContent("gs1/geography/ncert/class6/ch3/ch3", {"notes":{"geo6-ch3":{"era":"NCERT Class 6 — Geography, Chapter 3","significance":["Rotation and revolution, solstices and equinoxes are among the most consistently tested static geography facts in Prelims","Exact dates (21 June, 22 December, 21 March, 23 September) and hemisphere-specific season combinations are frequently asked as pair-matching questions","The Circle of Illumination, orbital plane inclination (66½°), and leap year logic are high-value recall facts"],"features":[{"key":"Two Motions of the Earth","main":"Rotation: movement of the Earth ON its axis\nRevolution: movement of the Earth AROUND the Sun in a fixed path (orbit)","note":""},{"key":"Axis and Orbital Plane","main":"The axis is an imaginary line making an angle of 66½° with the orbital plane\nThe orbital plane = the plane formed by the orbit of the Earth","note":"Axis is tilted at 66½° to the orbital plane (NOT 23½° — that is the tilt FROM the vertical/perpendicular to the orbital plane)"},{"key":"Day and Night (Rotation)","main":"Earth is spherical → only half faces the Sun at a time\nPortion facing Sun = day; portion away from Sun = night\nCircle of Illumination: the circle that divides day from night on the globe","note":"The Circle of Illumination does NOT coincide with the Earth's axis"},{"key":"Earthday","main":"Earth takes about 24 hours to complete one rotation on its axis\nThe period of rotation is known as the earthday\nThis is the daily motion of the Earth","note":""},{"key":"If Earth Did NOT Rotate","main":"The portion facing the Sun would always have day → continuous warmth\nThe other half would remain in permanent darkness and be freezing cold\nLife would not have been possible in such extreme conditions","note":""},{"key":"Revolution","main":"Earth takes 365¼ days (one year) to complete one revolution around the Sun\nWe consider a year as 365 days and ignore the 6 hours for convenience","note":"Earth travels in an elliptical orbit around the Sun"},{"key":"Leap Year","main":"6 hours saved every year are added over 4 years to make one extra day (24 hours)\nThis day is added to February → February has 29 days in a leap year\nA leap year has 366 days","note":""},{"key":"Earth's Inclination is Constant","main":"Throughout its orbit, the Earth remains inclined in the SAME DIRECTION\nThis constant inclination causes seasons","note":""},{"key":"Summer Solstice — 21 June","main":"On 21 June, the Northern Hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun\nSun's rays fall directly on the Tropic of Cancer (23½°N)\nAreas near the poles receive less heat\nNorth Pole is inclined towards the Sun → places beyond Arctic Circle get continuous daylight for ~6 months\nLongest day and shortest night in the Northern Hemisphere\nSummer in Northern Hemisphere; Winter in Southern Hemisphere","note":"This position of the Earth is called the Summer Solstice"},{"key":"Winter Solstice — 22 December","main":"On 22 December, the Tropic of Capricorn (23½°S) receives direct rays of the Sun\nSouth Pole tilts towards the Sun\nShorter days and longer nights in the Northern Hemisphere\nWinter in Northern Hemisphere; Summer in Southern Hemisphere\nChristmas is celebrated in Australia in the summer season","note":"This position is called the Winter Solstice"},{"key":"Spring Equinox — 21 March","main":"On 21 March, sun falls vertically on the equator\nNeither pole is tilted towards the Sun\nWhole Earth experiences equal days and equal nights\nThis is called an equinox\nSpring season in Northern Hemisphere; Autumn in Southern Hemisphere","note":""},{"key":"Autumn Equinox — 23 September","main":"On 23 September, sun again falls on the equator\nEqual days and equal nights everywhere\nAutumn in Northern Hemisphere; Spring in Southern Hemisphere","note":""},{"key":"Seasons Summary","main":"A year is divided into summer, winter, spring, and autumn seasons\nSeasons change due to the change in the position of the Earth around the Sun","note":""},{"key":"Aryabhata's Observation","main":"The ancient Indian astronomer Aryabhata had stated that 'the earth is round and rotates on its own axis'","note":"Testable 'Do You Know' fact from the chapter"}],"traps":[{"label":"66½° vs 23½° — Axis Tilt","lines":["Axis makes 66½° with the ORBITAL PLANE","Axis is tilted 23½° FROM the vertical/perpendicular to the orbital plane","Both describe the same tilt but expressed differently — UPSC tests both"]},{"label":"Solstice/Equinox Dates — Exact","lines":["Summer Solstice: 21 June","Winter Solstice: 22 December","Spring Equinox: 21 March","Autumn Equinox: 23 September"]},{"label":"Northern vs Southern Hemisphere Seasons","lines":["21 June: NH = Summer; SH = Winter","22 December: NH = Winter; SH = Summer","21 March: NH = Spring; SH = Autumn","23 September: NH = Autumn; SH = Spring","A common trap: swapping NH and SH seasons"]},{"label":"Circle of Illumination ≠ Axis","lines":["The Circle of Illumination (divides day from night) does NOT coincide with the Earth's axis","Common confusion — they are two different reference circles"]},{"label":"Which Tropic Gets Direct Sun When","lines":["Summer Solstice (21 June): Sun's rays direct on Tropic of CANCER (23½°N)","Winter Solstice (22 December): Sun's rays direct on Tropic of CAPRICORN (23½°S)","Equinoxes: Sun's rays direct on the EQUATOR (0°)"]},{"label":"Revolution Period","lines":["Actual: 365¼ days","Practical year: 365 days (6 hours ignored)","Every 4 years: 6 × 4 = 24 extra hours → added to February → 29 days → 366 days = Leap Year"]}],"mainsAngle":"The mechanism of seasons — caused by the Earth's constant axial tilt as it revolves around the Sun — explains why the two hemispheres always experience opposite seasons simultaneously. This has significant implications for agricultural cycles, monsoon patterns, and human settlement patterns globally. The Aryabhata fact also reflects India's ancient scientific tradition of understanding Earth's motions long before modern astronomy.","mainsTheme":"Earth's motions as the basis for seasons, time systems, and climatic patterns","recall":["Rotation = on axis (earthday = ~24 hrs); Revolution = around Sun (365¼ days)","Axis tilted at 66½° to orbital plane","Circle of Illumination: divides day from night; does NOT coincide with axis","Summer Solstice: 21 June → Sun on Tropic of Cancer → NH summer","Winter Solstice: 22 December → Sun on Tropic of Capricorn → NH winter","Equinox: equal day/night; 21 March (Spring NH) and 23 September (Autumn NH)","Leap year: 366 days; February has 29 days; every 4 years","Aryabhata: said Earth is round and rotates on its axis"]}},"quizzes":{"geo6-ch3":{"title":"Motions of the Earth","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes 'rotation' from 'revolution' of the Earth?","options":["Rotation: movement around the Sun; Revolution: movement on its axis","Rotation: movement on its axis; Revolution: movement around the Sun in a fixed orbit","Both refer to the same motion described differently","Rotation: daily motion; Revolution: motion caused by the Moon's gravity"],"correct":1,"explain":"Rotation = Earth spinning on its own axis (causing day/night). Revolution = Earth moving around the Sun in its orbit (causing seasons and the year)."},{"q":"The Earth's axis makes an angle of _____ with its orbital plane.","options":["23½°","45°","66½°","90°"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Earth's axis makes an angle of 66½° with its orbital plane. Note: it is tilted 23½° FROM the perpendicular to the orbital plane — both describe the same tilt."},{"q":"The 'Circle of Illumination' is best defined as:","options":["The equator's path around the Earth","The circle that divides day from night on the globe","Another name for the Earth's axis","The path of the Earth around the Sun"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Circle of Illumination is the circle that divides the day side from the night side on the globe. Critically, it does NOT coincide with the Earth's axis."},{"q":["Assertion (A): The Circle of Illumination does not coincide with the Earth's axis.","Reason (R): The Earth's axis is tilted relative to the orbital plane, causing the circle that separates day from night to cut across latitudes at a different angle than the axis.","Which one of the following is correct?"],"options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both are true and R correctly explains A — the axial tilt causes the Circle of Illumination to be at an angle to the axis, rather than coinciding with it."},{"q":"The period of one complete rotation of the Earth on its axis is known as:","options":["Earthyear","Earthday","Sidereal day","Solar month"],"correct":1,"explain":"The period of rotation (~24 hours) is known as the 'earthday' — the daily motion of the Earth."},{"q":"If the Earth did NOT rotate on its axis, which of the following would be the most likely consequence?","options":["Days and nights would be equal everywhere","One half of the Earth would experience continuous day (warmth) and the other continuous night (freezing cold), making life impossible","The Earth would revolve faster around the Sun","Seasons would occur more frequently"],"correct":1,"explain":"Without rotation, one hemisphere would face the Sun permanently (continuous day, extreme heat) while the other would be in permanent darkness (freezing cold) — conditions under which life would not be possible."},{"q":"The Earth takes approximately how long to complete one revolution around the Sun?","options":["365 days exactly","365¼ days","364 days","366 days"],"correct":1,"explain":"Earth takes 365¼ days (one year) to revolve around the Sun. We consider a year as 365 days for convenience, ignoring the extra 6 hours."},{"q":"Leap year occurs every 4 years because:","options":["The Moon's orbit around the Earth has a 4-year cycle","6 hours saved annually over 4 years add up to one full day (24 hours), which is added to February","The Earth's orbit around the Sun changes every 4 years","January gains an extra day every 4 years"],"correct":1,"explain":"6 hours × 4 years = 24 hours = 1 extra day, added to February — making it 29 days and the year 366 days in a leap year."},{"q":"The Earth travels around the Sun in which type of orbit?","options":["Circular","Elliptical","Spiral","Parabolic"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Earth travels around the Sun in an elliptical orbit, as visible in Figure 3.3 showing the Revolution of the Earth and Seasons."},{"q":"On 21 June (Summer Solstice), the Sun's rays fall directly on:","options":["The Equator","The Tropic of Capricorn (23½°S)","The Tropic of Cancer (23½°N)","The Arctic Circle (66½°N)"],"correct":2,"explain":"On 21 June, the Northern Hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun and the Sun's rays fall directly on the Tropic of Cancer (23½°N)."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about 21 June:","I. It is the longest day in the Northern Hemisphere.","II. It is the shortest night in the Northern Hemisphere.","III. The Southern Hemisphere experiences winter at this time.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I and II only","I, II and III","II and III only","I only"],"correct":1,"explain":"All three statements are correct — on 21 June (Summer Solstice), the Northern Hemisphere has its longest day and shortest night, while the Southern Hemisphere simultaneously experiences winter."},{"q":"On 22 December (Winter Solstice), which of the following is correct?","options":["Sun's rays fall directly on the Tropic of Cancer; Northern Hemisphere has summer","Sun's rays fall directly on the Tropic of Capricorn; Northern Hemisphere has winter and Southern Hemisphere has summer","Sun's rays fall on the Equator; equal days and nights everywhere","Sun's rays fall on the Arctic Circle; Northern Hemisphere has continuous daylight"],"correct":1,"explain":"On 22 December, the Sun's rays fall directly on the Tropic of Capricorn (23½°S). The Northern Hemisphere experiences winter while the Southern Hemisphere has summer."},{"q":"Christmas is celebrated in Australia during summer because:","options":["Australia is in the Northern Hemisphere and 22 December is the Summer Solstice","Australia is in the Southern Hemisphere, which experiences summer when the Northern Hemisphere has its winter (December)","Australia follows a different calendar from the rest of the world","The Southern Hemisphere is always warmer than the Northern Hemisphere"],"correct":1,"explain":"Australia is in the Southern Hemisphere. On 22 December (Northern Hemisphere's winter), the Southern Hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun — hence Australia experiences summer during Christmas."},{"q":"An equinox occurs when:","options":["The Sun is directly over the Tropic of Cancer","The whole Earth experiences equal days and equal nights, with the Sun falling vertically on the equator","One pole is tilted completely towards the Sun","The Earth is at its farthest point from the Sun"],"correct":1,"explain":"An equinox occurs when neither pole is tilted towards the Sun, the Sun falls on the equator, and the whole Earth experiences equal days and equal nights."},{"q":["Consider the following pairs:","Date — Event","I. 21 March — Autumn Equinox (Northern Hemisphere)","II. 23 September — Autumn Equinox (Northern Hemisphere)","III. 21 June — Winter Solstice (Northern Hemisphere)","IV. 22 December — Winter Solstice (Northern Hemisphere)","How many of the above pairs are correctly matched?"],"options":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","Only three pairs","All four pairs"],"correct":1,"explain":"Pairs II and IV are correctly matched. Pair I is incorrect (21 March is Spring Equinox for NH). Pair III is incorrect (21 June is Summer Solstice for NH, not winter)."},{"q":"On 21 March (Spring Equinox), which of the following is correct for the Southern Hemisphere?","options":["Summer","Spring","Autumn","Winter"],"correct":2,"explain":"When the Northern Hemisphere has Spring (21 March), the Southern Hemisphere simultaneously experiences Autumn — the opposite season."},{"q":["Assertion (A): Seasons change due to the change in the position of the Earth around the Sun.","Reason (R): The Earth's axis remains inclined in the same direction throughout its orbit, causing different parts of the Earth to receive varying amounts of sunlight at different times of year.","Which one of the following is correct?"],"options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both are true and R correctly explains A — the constant direction of the tilt combined with the orbital position is exactly the mechanism by which seasons change."},{"q":["Consider the following pairs (Position of Earth — Season in Northern Hemisphere):","I. Summer Solstice (21 June) — Summer","II. Winter Solstice (22 December) — Winter","III. Spring Equinox (21 March) — Autumn","IV. Autumn Equinox (23 September) — Autumn","How many of the above pairs are correctly matched?"],"options":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","Only three pairs","All four pairs"],"correct":1,"explain":"Pairs I and II are correctly matched. Pair III is incorrect (21 March is Spring, not Autumn, in the Northern Hemisphere). Pair IV is correct (23 September IS Autumn in NH). So actually I, II, and IV — three pairs."},{"q":"The ancient Indian astronomer who stated that 'the earth is round and rotates on its own axis' was:","options":["Brahmagupta","Varahamihira","Aryabhata","Bhaskaracharya"],"correct":2,"explain":"Aryabhata, the ancient Indian astronomer, had stated that 'the earth is round and rotates on its own axis' — a 'Do You Know' fact from the chapter."},{"q":["Read the following passage:","'What would happen if the earth did not rotate? The portion of the earth facing the sun would always experience day, thus bringing continuous warmth to the region. The other half would remain in darkness and be freezing cold all the time. Life would not have been possible in such extreme conditions.'","Which of the following is most directly supported by this passage?"],"options":["Earth's rotation is responsible for the change of seasons","Earth's rotation is essential for creating liveable temperature conditions by alternating day and night across the surface","Without rotation, the Earth would stop revolving around the Sun","The Moon's gravity would compensate if the Earth stopped rotating"],"correct":1,"explain":"The passage directly argues that rotation creates the day-night alternation that distributes heat and cold across the surface, making life possible — without it, extreme permanent conditions on each half would make life impossible."},{"q":"How many equinoxes does the Earth experience in a year, and on approximately which dates?","options":["One equinox — 21 June","Two equinoxes — 21 March and 23 September","Two equinoxes — 21 June and 22 December","Four equinoxes — one per season"],"correct":1,"explain":"There are two equinoxes per year: the Spring Equinox on 21 March and the Autumn Equinox on 23 September (from the Northern Hemisphere perspective)."},{"q":"On which date does the North Pole experience continuous daylight for about six months, as described in the chapter?","options":["22 December","23 September","21 March","21 June"],"correct":3,"explain":"On 21 June (Summer Solstice), the North Pole is inclined towards the Sun, and places beyond the Arctic Circle experience continuous daylight for about six months."}]}}});
